		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: What is the IF chip of MD-9600 V5?]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=5171&amp;p=37465#p37465"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div><blockquote class="uncited"><div><blockquote class="uncited"><div>The V5 radio uses two GT3136 IF chips.  These are conventional IF chips with normal filters. We don't have the schematic for these radios but the filters are switchable between narrow and wide. <br><br>Colin G4EML</div></blockquote>Thank you Colin, I am currently experiencing a problem, reading the audio buffer data of the MD-9600, I found that the received FSK audio data is sometimes overloaded, causing the decoding to fail, I want to see if there is a way to attenuate the input, I don't know if my description is accurate.</div></blockquote>The MCU has no control of the IF chips except to select the bandwidth of 12.5kHz or 25kHz, which is via a GPIO logic signal  Hight/Low.  There are no other adjustments.<br><br>Possibly the C6000 has some settings which control the input gain from the IF chip. but as you know the data sheet for the C6000 is not complete and there may not be a setting in the C6000 for this.</div></blockquote>I modified FMBpfOn for FM --<br>bandpass filter on, FSK decoding is working normally<br>//SPI0WritePageRegByte(0x04, 0x34, 0x3C);//Compressor off, Pre-Emph on 3KHz Audio Filter<br>SPI0WritePageRegByte(0x04, 0x34, 0xBC);//Compressor off, Pre-Emph on 3KHz Audio Filter on 2025-06-10<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=6826">BA7IQE</a> — Wed Jun 11, 2025 8:49 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
